  the host exists. Cache that tar on the laptop before Demo Day.

## 2026-09-02  Object storage provisioned and the D-020 denial verified against R2 (TICK-012, #20)

TICK-012 was closed on 2026-09-01 with only its code half done. The buckets did not exist,
and every test of `frontdoor.storage` had run against moto stubs. Reopened and reassigned;
this records the provisioning.

Cloudflare R2, free tier, account `b121ca93…`. Buckets `frontdoor-image` and
`frontdoor-depth`, two API tokens with distinct access keys.

    $ python -m frontdoor.storage verify        # moved by TICK-057 to frontdoor.storage_probe
    ok  images=frontdoor-image  depth=frontdoor-depth  loader-denied-on-depth

That is the acceptance criterion that says "verified by actually running it, not by reading
the policy", and it now holds against the provider rather than against a stub. Probed in
both directions:

    images credential -> depth bucket : DENIED (AccessDenied)
    depth  credential -> depth bucket : round trip OK
    depth  credential -> image bucket : DENIED

The third is stricter than D-020 requires -- the quarantine only asks that the LOADER cannot
read depth -- but it means neither token is a superset of the other, so a leaked one cannot
quietly become the other.

The live storage tests ran for the first time: 53 passed with FRONTDOOR_STORAGE_LIVE=1.

Confirmed in the Cloudflare dashboard on the same day:

- No payment method attached. D-026 depends on the free tier hard-stopping rather than
  billing quietly starting, and that only holds while no card is on the account.
- Neither bucket is publicly readable: Public Development URL disabled, no custom domain,
  on both. This is not a nicety. The sealed split lives in `frontdoor-image`, key names are
  `sealed/<capture_id>`, and `data/manifest.csv` will commit every capture_id to a public
  repo -- so a public bucket would let anyone reconstruct every sealed URL exactly. D-028
  records that the seal is a code-level refusal and not an access control; a public bucket
  is the hole underneath it.
- Bucket location is WNAM, read back from the provider rather than taken on trust:
  `get_bucket_location` returns 'WNAM' for `frontdoor-image`. Note that
  `FRONTDOOR_S3_REGION=auto` is a different thing -- the S3 API's region parameter, which is
  what R2 expects -- and says nothing about where the bucket lives. The two are easy to
  confuse and both are correct here.

The same call against `frontdoor-depth` with the images credential returns AccessDenied, so
the quarantine holds for metadata and not only for object bytes.

The evaluation host region remains open (#50). WNAM was chosen to match a North American
host; if #50 lands elsewhere, the mismatch is a latency question rather than a correctness
one, since R2 egress is free and the dataset is single-digit gigabytes.

## D-029  AMENDMENT TO D-014: the capture device type is the dual-wide, not the bare wide angle

Taken 2026-09-02, closing QA B01 on PR #196. This amends a locked decision, so it is logged
here and must be REPORTED AS AN AMENDMENT AT DEMO DAY (PRD section 2).

**What D-014 says.** Capture-only app on AVFoundation + CoreMotion, no ARKit, naming
`builtInWideAngleCamera` as the device and claiming it "yields true intrinsics, gravity,
LiDAR depth and full-resolution stills" (PRD.md section 4).

**What was measured.** TICK-020's on-device probe (#24), 2026-09-02, on two phones:
`builtInWideAngleCamera` delivers NO calibration data at all -- no intrinsics, no distortion
table -- and no depth. Intrinsics arrive only through `builtInDualWideCamera`. This is not a
device defect: Apple gates calibration delivery on depth or virtual-device constituent
delivery, and `AVCapturePhotoOutput.isCameraCalibrationDataDeliveryEnabled` requires two or
more constituent devices, which a single physical camera cannot satisfy. The device D-014
names cannot produce the data D-014 depends on, on any hardware.

**The amendment.** The capture device becomes `builtInDualWideCamera`, and the still is taken
from its 1x wide constituent. THE OPTICS D-014 FIXES ARE UNCHANGED: 1x main lens, no digital
zoom, no crop (ARCHITECTURE section 4). What changes is the AVFoundation device object used
to reach them, not the geometry of what is photographed, so no capture already specified is
invalidated and the error budget's premises are untouched.

**Also corrected in the same breath:** D-014's claim that this path yields LiDAR depth. It
does not. `builtInLiDARDepthCamera` delivers a depth map with no calibration data, so LiDAR
is not a route to intrinsics on any device; the depth that does arrive comes through the
dual-wide at `accuracy=relative`. See D-030's note on what that leaves open for D-020.

**Why it was owed and not taken.** The code moved to the dual-wide, ARCHITECTURE section 4
moved, and TEAM.md moved -- while the decision all three cite still described the wide-angle
path. ARCHITECTURE.md said the entry "is still owed"; the probe note said it needed "a team
decision, not a quiet code change"; TICK-020's own AC required it here. It was not written
for eleven days. That is the exact failure mode the decision log exists to prevent, and it is
recorded rather than quietly repaired.

PRD section 4's D-014 row is marked as amended and points here. Full sync of PRD section 4
against the log is TICK-116 (#116).

## D-030  Arm C is CUT. The Sep 2 gate lapsed and is decided here, two days late

Taken 2026-09-02, closing #43 (TICK-049) and QA B08 on PR #196.

**The decision: Arm C is cut.** Depth with intrinsics-only scaling and no reference object
(ARCHITECTURE section 5) will not be implemented. It is frozen in place rather than deleted:
`frontdoor.metrology` keeps `C` as a registered `PendingArm`, the ablation reports it as cut
with this reason, and the wire contract already carries the shape --
`measure_response.schema.json`'s `absent_reason: "cut"` names "Arm C on 2026-09-02 per D-013"
by example, written before the decision it anticipated.

**THIS IS NOT THE DECISION #43 ASKED FOR, and the difference matters.** TICK-049's acceptance
criteria require the decision to "cite Arm C's measured dev-split error, not an impression".
There is no measured error. Arm C is not implemented (#42 is open), the metrology library was
scaffolded only on 2026-09-02 (#194) with no geometry in it, and `data/manifest.csv` is
header-only -- no capture has ever been taken. The gate arrived with nothing to weigh.

So the cut is taken on the ground the gate was created to protect, which is scope and not
accuracy. PRD section 9 schedules it as "cut Arm C on Sep 2 if weak" precisely so that a
fourth arm cannot quietly consume the days the error budget, the conformal calibration and
the deck all need. With seven days to Demo Day, zero arms implemented and zero captures
taken, carrying a fourth is the risk the gate names. A cut on absence of evidence is weaker
than a cut on evidence, and saying so is part of the decision.

**What is lost, stated plainly.** Arm C carries the usability claim in D-013: it is the only
arm an unaided user can perform, needing no reference card. Cutting it means the accuracy
versus usability gradient in deliverable #4 is reported across A, A-prime and B only, and the
project can no longer say what "works with what you already carry" costs in accuracy. That is
a real reduction in what the work can claim, not a tidy-up.

**Consequence for R-10 and deliverable #5, which #43 does not cover.** TICK-020 also found
device depth arrives at `accuracy=relative` -- stereo disparity, not range. D-020's premise is
a monocular-versus-LiDAR comparison and deliverable #5 promises it on every entrance; a
relative map has no metric scale to compare against a caliper. That question is separate from
this cut, is NOT decided here, and needs its own entry.

**Not decided here either:** whether the 2026-09-01 pivot to plain-photo screening leaves any
of the four arms with an input at all, given it retires ROI taps and caliper ground truth. If
it does not, this cut is the smaller half of a much larger question, and A-3 (#67) is the
amendment ask that owns it.

## D-031  AMENDMENT TO D-026: the measure server is a PAID host. Object storage stays free.

**Amended 2026-09-04 by D-044.** The no-silent-billing mechanism is prepaid credits, not a Fly
spend limit. Original text below.

Taken 2026-09-02, closing the hosting question D-026 left open and unblocking TICK-062 (#50).
This amends a locked decision, so it is logged here and must be REPORTED AS AN AMENDMENT AT
DEMO DAY (PRD section 2).

**What D-026 says.** "All hosted infrastructure runs on PROVIDER FREE TIERS. No spend is
authorised for object storage or the server host." TICK-062's own acceptance criteria repeat
it: "the host is a free tier with no payment method exposed to overage".

**What changes.** The metrology server runs on **Fly.io**, `shared-cpu-1x` with 256 MB, one
machine held always-on, at roughly 2 US dollars per month. **Object storage does not change**
and stays on the R2 free tier exactly as D-026 requires -- this amendment is scoped to the
server host alone.

**Why free tiers do not work here, specifically.** The constraint is not cost, it is cold
starts. D-016 puts the demo in an interior atrium and defines a fallback chain because
cellular fails there. Every free always-on option was ruled out on evidence rather than
preference:

- Cloudflare, where R2 already lives, cannot run this image at all. The server is Flask and
  gunicorn on CPython 3.11; Workers are JS/WASM, Python Workers are Pyodide and cannot carry
  arbitrary dependencies, and Cloudflare Containers require the paid Workers plan.
- Render's free tier spins down and takes roughly fifty seconds to wake. That is the demo
  failing on stage, which is precisely the risk D-016 exists to manage.
- Oracle Always Free would work and costs nothing, but the team owns no domain and no TLS
  certificate, and iOS App Transport Security requires HTTPS from the phone. That trades two
  dollars for a DNS-and-certificate failure mode on the one day it cannot fail.

**Why Fly rather than Render's paid tier.** Measured, not estimated: the image serves
`GET /health` while capped at 256 MB and uses **69 MiB**. Fly's smallest always-on machine is
256 MB at about 2 dollars; Render's cheapest is 512 MB at 7. Fly also pins the region -- `sjc`,
near the WNAM buckets -- and runs health checks against the `/health` endpoint the server
already has.

**D-026's other clause survives and is not waived.** "Billing must be incapable of starting
silently." On a paid plan that is no longer satisfied by having no card, so it is replaced by
an explicit spend limit on the Fly organisation plus a billing alert, both recorded in
`docs/server-deploy.md`. A free tier that hard-stops and a paid plan with a cap are different
mechanisms for the same requirement; the requirement is unchanged.

**Scope, stated so it is not read too widely.** This authorises the server host and nothing
else. Object storage stays free (D-026). No GPU, no depth model on the host: the live image
serves Arms A and A-prime only, Arm B reports `unavailable`, and Arm C reports `cut` (D-030).
The image is identical to the one the laptop fallback runs, which is what makes step 3 of
D-016's chain a fallback rather than a different system.

**Consequence for TICK-062 (#50).** Its acceptance criterion naming a free tier is superseded
by this entry. The criteria it does NOT waive -- same image digest on host and laptop,
credentials by environment variable, `/health` answered from a phone on cellular, image cached
on the laptop before Demo Day -- all stand.

**Deployed 2026-09-02.** https://frontdoor-measure.fly.dev, machine `0803444b2dd568` in `sjc`,
image digest `sha256:a20ca31a...fb637a`, 72 MB. Spend limit and billing alert set before the first
deploy, satisfying D-026's no-silent-billing clause by cap rather than by absence of a card.
Verified live: `/health` 200 over HTTPS, plain HTTP 301s to TLS (iOS ATS), and `POST /measure`
returns a response that validates against the frozen schema with Arm A and A-prime measured, Arm B
`unavailable` and Arm C `cut`. Runbook: `docs/server-deploy.md`.

**Still open, and NOT decided here:** whether the evaluation harness runs on this host or
locally on a team Mac. D-026 lists it as required before #50 closes. A 256 MB machine cannot
score a few hundred captures through four arms, so the answer is almost certainly local -- but
that contradicts the "bucket beside the VM" premise in ARCHITECTURE sections 7-8 and needs its
own entry rather than being assumed here.

## D-032  LiDAR depth is captured, on James's iPhone 17 Pro (`iPhone18,1`)

**Amended 2026-09-04 by D-045.** Captured depth is relative stereo disparity, not LiDAR range.
Original text below.

Taken 2026-09-02 by David. Reverses the 2026-09-01 pivot's "no LiDAR depth" clause for
capture; everything else in the pivot stands.

**The decision.** Every entrance is captured with LiDAR depth (D-020), and the device that
carries it is **James's iPhone 17 Pro (`iPhone18,1`)**. One LiDAR device is enough: D-020 asks for depth on
every entrance, not on every device, so a single phone that shoots every entrance satisfies it.

**What this does NOT claim, because the probe measured otherwise.** TICK-020 (#24) ran
`builtInLiDARDepthCamera` on an iPhone 15 Pro Max on 2026-09-02: depth is delivered, calibration
is **not**, and intrinsics are not delivered. The depth that arrives through the dual-wide is
`accuracy=relative`. So LiDAR is a depth map to store and compare against, NOT a route to
intrinsics and NOT a metric ruler. D-015 is untouched: the metrology method still never consumes
device depth. This entry authorises capture and storage of depth, nothing further.

**The device is an iPhone 17 Pro, not the iPhone 16 Pro the roster recorded.** Read from
`devicectl` on 2026-09-02 (#203, Emily). This entry was first written against the audited model
and is corrected here rather than quietly: probe rows are keyed by model identifier, so a depth
row filed against the wrong one would prove nothing about the phone actually shooting the dataset.

**Two prerequisites that are real and are not waived.** A build **has** run on that phone
(installed and launched 2026-09-02), correcting an earlier claim in TEAM.md section 2. What is
still missing is its **capability-probe row**: its depth delivery is expected, not measured, and TICK-020
should be run on it before it carries a capture day. Separately, James runs Windows, so the
mandatory 7-day re-sign (D-025) is a physical session with a Mac owner. Both were raised before
this decision was taken and are recorded here rather than discovered in the field.

**Consequence for TEAM.md section 3.** The constraint "field capture runs on Emily's two phones,
not James's" is reopened by this entry: capture on the LiDAR device means capture through James's
phone, which is the arrangement TICK-020 had retired. Who operates which device is not settled
here.

## D-033  AMENDMENT TO D-020 and D-031: the server holds a WRITE-ONLY depth token

Taken 2026-09-02 by David, to let TICK-029 (#33) upload depth without breaking the quarantine.

**The problem.** D-031 gives the server the images credential ONLY, never depth. But #33 requires
depth to reach the quarantined bucket, and the chosen upload path (option 2) routes bytes through
the server. A read-capable depth token on the server would put depth where the metrology path can
reach it, which is exactly what D-020 forbids.

**The decision.** The server holds a third token, scoped **Object Write only** on
`frontdoor-depth` -- no read, no list. It can store a depth map and can never read one back.

**Why this satisfies D-020 rather than eroding it.** D-020's guarantee is that the metrology code
path cannot *read* depth: "if depth sits where the method can reach it, it eventually gets used to
tune." A write-only credential cannot be used to tune, to peek, or to load. The evaluation harness
keeps the read token and remains the only reader. The invariant is unchanged; only the direction
of the server's access is.

**What is now true of each credential:**

    server / loader    frontdoor-image   read + write
    server             frontdoor-depth   WRITE ONLY  (this entry)
    harness            frontdoor-image   read
    harness            frontdoor-depth   read
    metrology library  neither           no credential at all

**Verification.** `python -m frontdoor.storage verify` *(TICK-057)* must still print `loader-denied-on-depth`.
*(Command moved 2026-09-02 by TICK-057 to `python -m frontdoor.storage_probe verify` -- the
probe reads depth, so it cannot live in the module the loader and the server import for
images. The old spelling now refuses loudly instead of exiting 0 in silence.)*
A new check asserts the server's depth token can PUT and is REFUSED on GET; if a read succeeds,
the write-only scope leaked and this entry's guarantee is void.

## D-034  The capture sidecar carries its mode: metrology, screening, or imported

Taken 2026-09-02 by David, closing TICK-027 (#31). This changes a FROZEN CONTRACT -- the
capture sidecar schema -- so it is logged here rather than made as a quiet code change.

**The problem.** The 2026-09-01 pivot made capture plain photos: no caliper, no card, no
ROI taps. The app implemented the pre-pivot study and refused to save anything else. Three
gates, each of them correct for metrology and each fatal for screening: the camera sat
behind a caliper reading, `CaptureWriter` refused a capture with no ROI taps, and the
schema required `ground_truth`, `card_placement` and `intrinsics`. So an operator
following `docs/capture-protocol.md` literally could not produce a record at all, which
is the likeliest reason #67 still reads 0/40 entrances.

**The decision.** One sidecar schema with a `capture_mode` discriminator, not two schemas:

    metrology   the pre-registered study. Caliper truth, ROI taps, card placement,
                intrinsics, gravity -- everything it always required, unchanged.
    screening   the plain-photo protocol. Our camera, so intrinsics and gravity are
                recorded WHEN THE DEVICE OFFERS THEM and omitted when it does not;
                no caliper, no card, no taps, no surface.

                Optional rather than required, corrected during review: the phone
                carrying depth capture (D-032) has never been through the capability
                probe, and a protocol that refused outright on a device delivering no
                distortion table would lose the entrance rather than record it. A lost
                doorway is worse than a sidecar with a gap. Metrology still REQUIRES
                the camera model, because every arm depends on it.
    imported    a photo taken outside this app. None of our capture metadata exists
                for it and none is invented.

**Absent means metrology.** Every sidecar written before this entry stays valid and stays
held to its original contract. The loosening does not reach backwards.

**The forbidding is the point, not the optionality.** A screening capture may not CARRY a
caliper reading, and an imported photo may not carry intrinsics -- the schema refuses
them rather than merely allowing them to be missing. A plausible number in one of those
fields would be read downstream as a measurement, and the error analysis treats device,
lens and intrinsics as facts. A gap that says "nobody measured this" is worth more than a
value nobody looked at.

**What else moved, and why each one is a correctness fix rather than a loosening:**

- `conditions.surface` is optional. `docs/capture-protocol.md` never asks a screening
  operator to record it, and a picker with a default is a value nobody observed.
- **R-3's 3 m distance cap is metrology-only.** The protocol asks for a "far, ~3-4 m" shot
  to show the approach path; the cap would have refused the shot the protocol requires.
  ConditionTags' own note predicted exactly this ("a hard cap here would silently reject
  the screening shots"). The cap still binds for metrology, where the Arm A error budget
  is the reason it exists.
- The condition-tag vocabulary is reconciled. The protocol offered "dusk/low light" and
  the app had no such tag, so an operator following it had to pick a wrong one; `lowLight`
  is added. The protocol listed occluders ("parked vehicle, scaffolding") where the app
  records a degree (none/partial/heavy); the protocol now asks for the degree and gives
  those as examples. One vocabulary, named identically in both places.
- An imported photo whose file cannot say WHEN it was taken, or WHAT took it, is refused.
  Dating it to the import time would put a wrong answer in the field that records when the
  entrance was seen, and a wrong answer is worse than a missing photo.

**Two leaks found in review, both worth recording because they are the failure this entry
claims to prevent.** First, `conditions.surface` was moved out of the shared required list
and not restored for metrology, so a metrology capture -- and every sidecar written before
this entry -- could drop it and still validate. The loosening reached backwards, which is
exactly what "absent means metrology" promises it cannot. Second, `EntranceStore` returns
an entrance already recorded WITH its caliper reading, deliberately, so a doorway shot in
metrology in the morning and screening in the afternoon wrote `ground_truth` into a
screening sidecar. The writer now gates those fields on the MODE rather than on whether
the entrance happens to carry them, and the same applies to `card_placement` and
`surface`. Both are pinned by tests.

**An entrance first recorded by a screening capture can later take a reading.** Without
that it could never be captured in metrology mode on that device: the setup screen reused
the reading-less entrance and the writer refused it, with no way to supply one. The
no-overwrite rule still stands where it was meant to -- an entrance that already has a
reading keeps it against a second operator.

**Metrology is not deleted.** Its path stays in the app behind a mode switch, and every
one of its gates still fires. Whether that study is alive is A-3 (#67), an open team
question this entry deliberately does not answer -- it makes the app able to run the
protocol the field is actually running, without deciding that the other one is dead.

**What this does NOT do.** It does not enforce the 5-6 view shot list, which was TICK-027's
original subject. That plan was written for card placements and the A-prime subset, both
gone with the pivot; the view set now lives in the protocol and is followed by hand.
Re-prescribing it in the instrument is worth doing and is not done here.

## A-3  AMENDMENT TO THE PRE-REGISTRATION: one product, on iPhone Pro with LiDAR

Taken 2026-09-02 by David. This is the amendment James asked for on #67 on 2026-09-01 and
again on 2026-09-02. It amends PRD section 2, so it MUST BE REPORTED AS AN AMENDMENT AT
DEMO DAY.

**The decision. There is one product**, and its target device is an **iPhone Pro with
LiDAR**. Everything that exists to serve a different device or a different route to scale
is a LATER VERSION and is deprioritised: it is not dead, and it is not competing for this
week.

**What that resolves.** Since the 2026-09-01 pivot the repository has carried two lines of
work -- the plain-photo screening stack (engine, /screen, ground-truth labels, Street View
pre-catalogue, map) and the metrology arms with their caliper truth and reference card.
Both were being invested in, and both cannot be the Demo Day story. The screening product
is the product. The metrology arms are the later version.

**Consequence for the pre-registered hypothesis, stated plainly.** PRD section 2 pre-
registers MAE <= 0.25" on threshold rise, against caliper ground truth, on the sealed test
split, judged on Arm A. That study is deprioritised by this entry, so **the primary
hypothesis is not tested in this window.** It is not relaxed, not re-scoped and not
re-judged against a different arm -- it is untested, and reporting it as untested is the
only honest option available. D-007 exists so that this is said out loud rather than
replaced by a number from too few entrances.

**What survives from the metrology work, and is reported as findings rather than
discarded:** the rise-error-versus-angle budget (#35, #138, #159) was derived BEFORE any
data existed, and stands as a predicted error budget -- deliverable #3 in its predictive
half. The seal machinery (D-017, D-028) is exercised by the screening study, which carries
its own sealed split from the same committed seed.

**Deliverables this changes.** #1 becomes photographs with operator presence labels rather
than caliper truth. #4 (arm ablation) and #5 (monocular versus LiDAR) are not delivered in
this window; both belong to the later version. #2, #6 and #7 stand.

**LiDAR is CAPTURED, not CONSUMED, and this entry does not change that.** Targeting Pro
hardware with a depth sensor does not make depth an input to the method: D-015's boundary
holds, and D-020's quarantine holds with it. LiDAR is recorded per D-032 for the later
comparison, and the screening engine does not read it.

**The device pool is TWO, not one.** TEAM.md section 2 records "LiDAR-capable devices: 1".
That is wrong: every iPhone 15 Pro has a LiDAR scanner, so Emily's iPhone 15 Pro Max
qualifies as well -- and it is the device TICK-020 actually ran `builtInLiDARDepthCamera`
on. So the target device for this product is held by two people, one of whom has a Mac and
a probed phone. Corrected in TEAM.md by this entry.

**A-2 attribution:** resolved by #132 from commit 13e735a. A-2 is the pre-registration amendment;
D-022 is its decision-register cross-reference.

## D-035  The evaluation harness runs on a team Mac, not on the server host

Taken 2026-09-02 by David, closing the second decision TICK-062 (#50) requires and the
question D-031 explicitly left open.

**Why it needed an entry.** Not because nothing backed it -- something did, and getting
that wrong was the first draft of this entry. The 2026-09-01 entry *Server image, live
arms, harness location (TICK-062, #50)* already recorded "Evaluation harness runs on a team
Mac, against the same R2 buckets, not on the server VM". Then D-031, the next day, said the
question was "Still open, and NOT decided here". So the log CONTRADICTED ITSELF, and
ARCHITECTURE sections 7-8 followed the earlier entry while the later one said no decision
existed. This entry settles which is operative: the 2026-09-01 text was right, D-031's
"still open" is withdrawn, and the reason is recorded below rather than left implicit.

**The decision.** The harness runs locally on a team Mac, reading the dataset from R2 over
the network. The server host runs the request path only: `/health`, `/measure`, `/screen`,
`/upload` and the map.

**Why.** The host is one 256 MB machine with a single gunicorn worker and two threads. It
is sized for one request at a time, not for scoring a few hundred captures; and the team
has three Macs sitting idle for that purpose. Putting the harness there would also give the
host a reason to hold the depth READ credential, which D-020 and D-033 exist to prevent --
the harness is the only reader of depth, and it should not live where the request path
lives.

**What this supersedes.** D-031's "Still open, and NOT decided here" clause on the harness
question, and ARCHITECTURE sections 7-8's original "bucket beside the VM" premise. That
architecture text was corrected on 2026-09-01; this entry is the decision it now cites.

**Consequence, stated so it is not discovered later.** The harness reads from R2 over the
network rather than from a local disk beside the bucket. Egress on R2 is free, so this
costs nothing in money; it costs time on a slow link, and the unsealing run (#63) is the
one execution where that matters. Whoever runs it should sync the dataset before Sep 7
rather than streaming it on the day.

## D-036  One capture device, LiDAR on, no caliper

**Amended 2026-09-04 by D-045.** Clause 1 restated: depth is captured; it is stereo disparity,
not LiDAR range. Original text below.

Taken 2026-09-02 by David, in the backlog sync that follows A-3. Three settings that go
together, recorded as one entry because separating them would hide what each costs.

**1. LiDAR is used.** Confirming D-032 rather than repeating it: depth is captured on every
entrance. What D-032 says still bounds it -- TICK-020 measured that LiDAR delivers depth
with NO calibration, and that dual-wide depth is `accuracy=relative`. So depth is a map to
store and compare against, not a metric ruler, and D-015's method boundary is untouched:
the method still never consumes device depth.

**2. Capture runs on ONE device: James's iPhone Pro with LiDAR.** Not a pool. Emily's
iPhone 16 and iPhone 15 Pro Max stop being capture devices for this dataset.

Reasons to record, because both directions have a cost:
- Depth on every entrance (D-020) is only satisfiable by a device with a depth sensor, and
  one operator with one phone is the shortest path to a uniform dataset.
- `device_model` stops being a stratification variable and becomes a CONSTANT. A-1 already
  needed restating for a two-device pool; with one device the generalisation limit is
  sharper, not softer, and the findings must say the result is measured on a single phone.
- **The single point of failure is real and is not waived.** Free-provisioning builds expire
  after 7 days (D-025, R-7), James runs Windows and cannot re-sign unaided, and that phone
  has still never been through the capability probe (#24). If it expires, breaks or turns
  out not to deliver what the probe assumes, capture stops -- there is no second device
  shooting alongside it. Probing it is now urgent rather than tidy.

**MODEL, RESOLVED 2026-09-02.** This entry was first written flagging a discrepancy: the
decision was given as "iPhone Pro Max" while #203 had measured James's device as **iPhone 17
Pro (`iPhone18,1`)**. There was no discrepancy -- the two names are two different phones.
**James holds an iPhone Pro; Emily holds an iPhone Pro Max. Both carry LiDAR.** The measured
identifier was right, and treating it as the anchor over the marketing name was the correct
call.

**This changes what the single-device choice IS.** A-3's correction had already established
that two devices qualify as the target hardware, and this confirms it: Emily's Pro Max has
a LiDAR scanner, and it is the device TICK-020 actually ran `builtInLiDARDepthCamera` on --
so it is the PROBED one of the two. Capture on James's phone alone is therefore a
**deliberate constraint, not a hardware limit**. One operator with one phone gives a uniform
dataset and a constant `device_model`, which is the reason to keep it.

**The single point of failure now has a mitigation, and it should be used rather than
admired.** If James's build expires (D-025, 7 days), or his phone turns out not to deliver
what the probe assumes, **Emily's Pro Max is a qualifying second device**: same target class,
LiDAR present, already probed, and owned by someone with a Mac who can re-sign unaided.
Switching mid-dataset would make `device_model` a two-level variable rather than a constant,
which the findings would have to state -- but that is a far smaller cost than a capture week
that stops. Deciding to switch is a call for the day it is needed; recording that the option
exists is this entry's job.

CORRECTION 2026-09-02, confirmed by Emily: **the Pro Max is borrowed and has already been
returned.** It is not hers, it comes back only intermittently by arrangement with its owner,
and there is no fixed schedule. The paragraph above is left standing as written, because the
capability half of it is right -- that handset is the probed one, LiDAR is present, and Emily
has the Mac. What is wrong is "owned by", and availability is the half a mitigation needs.

So the option this entry records **cannot be exercised on demand**. Invoking it means asking
someone outside the team for a phone on the day James's build expires or his device
disappoints the probe. Emily's other handset, the iPhone 16, is non-Pro with no LiDAR and does
not satisfy clause 1 of this decision.

The single point of failure should therefore be read as **unmitigated until the borrow is
arranged for a defined window**. That is a call for whoever owns the schedule, not something a
correction settles. What it does change today: the capability probe on James's phone (#24) is
no longer merely urgent -- it is the only check standing in front of a capture week with no
working instrument, and it is the one thing on this list that costs nothing but an appointment.

**3. No caliper. There is no instrument ground truth.** SUPERSEDES **D-003**, which made a
digital caliper at +/-0.01" the ground-truth instrument for threshold rise.

What this settles rather than defers: A-3 said the metrology study was deprioritised. This
says it cannot be run at all in this window, because its ground truth does not exist and
will not be collected. The pre-registered criterion in PRD section 2 -- MAE <= 0.25" against
caliper ground truth -- has no measurable subject. It is untested, and reported as untested.

Consequences, each stated so none is discovered later:
- Deliverable #1 becomes photographs with **operator presence labels**, not caliper truth.
- Deliverable #5, the monocular-versus-LiDAR comparison, has no reference to compare
  against and is not delivered in this window. R-10's bench test likewise.
- The sidecar's `ground_truth` is never populated. D-034 already made it optional and made
  it FORBIDDEN on a screening capture, so no code change is required -- but the app's
  metrology mode is now inert for this study, since no reading exists to enter. The mode
  stays in the app per A-3; nothing this week can exercise it.
- The screening study's ground truth is unaffected: it is the operator's presence labels
  (#168), recorded at the door, and needs no instrument.

## D-037  The sidecar records how its pixels are stored

Taken 2026-09-02 by Emily, fixing forward from the #51 device round trip. Additive to the
capture sidecar contract: `image.exif_orientation`, an integer 1-8, required.

**What was wrong.** The app writes the camera's own encoded bytes (D-018, TICK-028 AC2). On a
portrait-held phone those bytes are a LANDSCAPE sensor buffer carrying EXIF tag 274 = 6, which
tells a viewer to turn it a quarter turn. Confirmed on the three captures pulled off the
borrowed iPhone 15 Pro Max: 4032x3024 stored, tagged 6.

Every pixel quantity in the sidecar -- `image.width`/`height`, `intrinsics` fx/fy/cx/cy,
`distortion_center`, and the `roi` points -- is expressed in that stored grid, with the rotation
NOT applied. The sidecar never said so, and never recorded the tag.

**Why that matters more than it looks.** The metrology package is handed a DECODED image
(`metrology/arm.py`); the loader returns bytes (`loader.py`) and nothing in between fixes a
frame. So the decode happens outside every test that exists. `cv2.imread` applies EXIF
orientation by default. A consumer using it gets a 3024x4032 array while `cx`/`cy` still
describe the 4032x3024 one -- and the result is not an exception, it is a plausible rise, wrong
in the same direction on every capture. That is R-11's failure exactly: the demo and the error
budget would agree with each other and both be wrong.

Nothing in the record made it detectable. `image.width`/`height` could have, but no rule said
which frame was authoritative, so a mismatch read as a bug in the check.

**Required rather than optional**, which is the whole point: an absent field would be read as
"upright", and upright is precisely the value that is wrong for the case that actually occurs.
Costless today -- `data/manifest.csv` is empty, so no committed capture is invalidated.

**Rejected alternative: strip the tag at write time** so no reader can rotate. Simpler, and it
removes the ambiguity instead of describing it. Rejected because it makes every capture display
sideways to the humans reviewing the dataset (#66's foldered photo sets, the Demo Day deck) and
discards which way was up, which the gravity vector only partly recovers. Recording is
reversible; stripping is not.

Baking the rotation into the pixels was rejected for a stronger reason: it would require
transforming the intrinsics to match, by hand, which is the same class of error one layer down.

The depth map does not carry the field. Depth is `{path, sha256}` and nothing else; it has no
EXIF, and that object is `additionalProperties: false` -- a field riding along on a shared type
is the shape QA B01 already caught once.
## 2026-09-02 — Signals, credentials and the memory floor (TICK-062, issue #50)

Three properties of the image itself, none of which D-031 covers: it records where the
image runs, these record how it behaves once it is there.

- gunicorn now runs as PID 1. The shell-form `CMD` left `sh` holding PID 1, so a stop
  signal killed the shell and the container went down with in-flight requests still open.
  D-016's fallback chain stops and starts this container while a phone may be uploading.
  Now `CMD ["sh", "-c", "exec gunicorn …"]`, with the shell kept only to expand `$PORT`.
- The absence of credentials in the image is asserted, not assumed: no `FRONTDOOR_*` in the
  image config and no `.env` in any layer. A real `.env` sits in the working tree and
  python-dotenv is a runtime dependency, so a loosened `.dockerignore` would have shipped
  live R2 keys into an image on a public host. Verified against a deliberately leaky build.
- The memory floor is far below the machine we bought. D-031 measured 69 MiB at the 256 MB
  cap; below that, the server still serves four concurrent 12 MB uploads on `-m 24m` and is
  OOM-killed at `-m 16m`. Werkzeug spools a large file part to disk rather than holding it
  in memory, so upload size barely moves the resident set. The committed test runs the
  four-way case at the deployed machine's 256 MB, which makes it a fit check against the
  real instance rather than a measurement of the ceiling.

The container's response is also now validated against the frozen schema, rather than
checked with a few ad-hoc assertions -- what the ticket's Testing section asked for, since
the flask-client tests validate the source tree and not what the image puts on the wire.

## D-038  AMENDMENT TO #187: R2 has no object versioning; the sealed partition is LOCKED instead

Taken 2026-09-02 while implementing #187, which asked for object versioning on
`frontdoor-image` and `frontdoor-depth` so that deleting a sealed object would be
recoverable rather than merely detectable.

**Versioning cannot be had.** `PutBucketVersioning` and `GetBucketVersioning` are on R2's
unimplemented list and there is no `ListObjectVersions`. A delete against these buckets is
final and there is nothing to restore from, so #187's premise -- and its delete-then-restore
check -- do not survive contact with the provider.

**What replaces it.** R2 bucket locks refuse deletes AND overwrites under a key prefix, for a
period or indefinitely, and apply to objects already stored as well as to new ones. That makes
losing a sealed object impossible rather than recoverable, which is the stronger property and
the one D-007 actually needs: the sealed split is opened once, on 2026-09-07, and there is no
second attempt.

**Scoped to `sealed/`, not the bucket.** A bucket-wide rule breaks the credential probe
(`python -m frontdoor.storage_probe verify`, moved there by TICK-057), which writes and
deletes `open/_frontdoor_probe` on every run.
Locking the whole bucket would trade the D-020 and D-033 denials for a probe key nobody needs
kept.

**The check changes shape.** There being no restore, the confirmation is not
delete-then-restore but delete-then-still-there: put a throwaway key under `sealed/`, delete
it, read it back. The delete's return value proves nothing; the read-back does.

**Consequence.** `ObjectStore.delete()` stays deliberately unguarded. A refusal in our own code
does not stop a console click, an SDK script holding the images token, or a token that outlives
the sprint, which is most of the ways the split actually gets lost -- the control belongs at the
provider. Note also that locked bytes cannot be deleted, so `sealed/` becomes one-way against
D-026's 10 GB cap; projected volume is 2-5 GB and only that prefix is locked.

Not yet applied: the rule needs Cloudflare dashboard access. `data/STORAGE.md` carries the
procedure and a marked placeholder for the transcript. #187's title and its versioning criteria
need rewriting to match this entry.

