## 2026-09-03 — Correct the depth-ingest boundary (TICK-249/TICK-250, #216/#217)

## D-039  Depth ingest is isolated behind an authenticated Worker

Taken 2026-09-03 after checking D-033 against Cloudflare's actual R2 permissions.
**Supersedes D-033's mechanism, not D-020's quarantine requirement.**

Cloudflare R2 permanent API tokens offer Object Read & Write or Object Read only. There is no
permanent Object Write-only token to provision, so D-033 described a provider control that did
not exist. Reusing the harness's read/write credential on Fly would make the metrology request
path capable of reading depth and would silently void the reason for the quarantine.

The replacement is a service boundary. Fly continues to authenticate the phone, derive the
partition, enforce the size ceiling, and verify the received SHA-256. It then forwards depth to
an authenticated PUT-only Cloudflare Worker. The Worker writes through an R2 binding and exposes
no read route. Fly receives no R2 depth credential; the evaluation harness retains the only R2
credential used to read depth.

This is a correction rather than a relaxation: the original no-read outcome remains, while the
unprovisionable `FRONTDOOR_DEPTH_WRITE_*` pair and its misleading probe are removed.

## 2026-09-03 — The depth-ingest Worker is deployed and verified against production R2 (TICK-251, issue #218)

TICK-249 built the Worker and TICK-250 routed the server through it; both were verified against
stubs. This is the same code against the real bucket, which is the only place the R2 binding, the
encrypted secret and Cloudflare's edge behaviour actually exist.

`frontdoor-depth-ingest` is deployed on the account that owns the buckets, bound to
`frontdoor-depth` through `DEPTH_BUCKET`, with `FRONTDOOR_DEPTH_INGEST_KEY` set as an encrypted
Worker secret. The server still holds no R2 depth credential: it has a URL and a service key, and
the capability to write depth lives entirely inside the Worker. D-020's quarantine is unchanged.

Verified live rather than assumed. An authenticated PUT of an `open/` probe returned 201; the bytes
read back through `frontdoor.depth_access` with a matching SHA-256; repeating the PUT returned 409
and left the stored object untouched; a bad credential returned 401 and created no object; `GET`
returned 405. The probe was deleted afterwards and the bucket's `open/` prefix left empty.

The probe key is under `open/` deliberately. #187 locked `sealed/` against deletion indefinitely,
so a sealed probe object could never have been cleaned up -- the two tickets constrain each other
and the ordering only works one way.

**A trap worth recording, because it costs an hour and reads as a Worker bug.** Calling the Worker
with `urllib.request` returns `403 error code: 1010` -- Cloudflare's edge refusing the
`Python-urllib/3.x` User-Agent before the Worker runs. Nothing reaches the code, so the Worker logs
show nothing and the response looks like a rejected credential. `frontdoor_server.depth_ingest`
uses `http.client`, which sends no User-Agent and is not blocked; the production path was never
affected. `docs/server-deploy.md` now says so next to the deploy steps.

The runbook's command order was also wrong: it had `wrangler secret put` before `wrangler deploy`,
and the secret attaches to a Worker that already exists. Corrected to deploy first.

## 2026-09-03 — Re-record the sidecar digests over the bytes git actually stores (TICK-092, #236)

The 48 `sidecar_sha256` values recorded by the TICK-092 ingest matched no checkout anywhere.
`append_capture` hashed exactly the file on disk — no ingest step wrote bytes different from the
ones it hashed — but the ingest machine's working tree was CRLF, `.gitattributes` pinned only
`data/manifest.csv`, and `core.autocrlf` rewrote every sidecar to LF between working tree and
object store. The manifest sealed byte sequences that ceased to exist at `git add`. The #236
review carries the forensic diagnosis: 0/48 committed blobs match the manifest, 48/48 CRLF
renderings do, and the integrity test's verdict flipped with the reader's `autocrlf` setting.

Two changes, one commit:

- `data/sidecars/*.json text eol=lf` in `.gitattributes`, so working tree equals blob on every
  machine and #236's checks mean one thing everywhere.
- The 48 `sidecar_sha256` cells re-recorded from the LF blob bytes. Only that column changed;
  `image_sha256` and `depth_sha256` are untouched (photos and depth are repo-external and never
  passed through eol translation).

This is not the digest-recomputing #236's docstring warns against. That warning guards against
silencing a symptom before the divergent step is found; here the step is found — git's eol
translation, not a re-serialisation or a staging path — and the old digests describe bytes that
exist in no checkout, so the record was wrong about every row it exists to seal (D-017, D-018).
Re-recording the same semantic content under the normalization the repo now enforces, with the
diagnosis linked, is the correction the append-only discipline is meant to produce.

Verified against the object store rather than any working tree: sha256 of each staged sidecar
blob (`git cat-file blob :data/sidecars/<id>.json`) equals its manifest cell, 48/48, and #236's
four checks pass against the corrected data.

