## D-045  AMENDMENT TO D-032 and D-036: captured depth is stereo disparity, not LiDAR range

Taken 2026-09-04. The 2026-09-04 capability probe on James's iPhone 17 Pro (`iPhone18,1`) measured
the capture path D-032 and D-036 named (PR #255, `docs/tick-020-capability-probe.md`).

D-032 authorised LiDAR depth on every entrance ("LiDAR depth is captured"; "Every entrance is
captured with LiDAR depth"). D-036 clause 1 said "LiDAR is used." What the probe recorded on
that phone:

- Normal capture uses `builtInDualWideCamera` because it is the only device that delivers the
  intrinsics `CaptureValidation.record` requires.
- The depth that arrives is `kCVPixelFormatType_DisparityFloat16` with
  `AVDepthData.Accuracy.relative`: relative stereo disparity, persisted as `DepthFloat32`.
- `builtInLiDARDepthCamera` delivers depth without calibration, so the app does not use that
  path. The probe did not observe LiDAR range.

D-040 already recorded this while fixing the phone as the sole device. This entry amends the two
decisions whose wording was wrong: depth is captured and stored; it is not LiDAR range and it is
not a metric ruler. D-015 and D-020 are untouched — screening still never consumes device depth.
The planned monocular-versus-LiDAR comparison has no metric reference.

**Reported as an amendment at Demo Day.**

